WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.820
Can a laptop in the office
reach our production line today?

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:05.830
Four ways the plant stops.

00:00:07.117 --> 00:00:09.687
First, the office network
reaches the plant.

00:00:09.992 --> 00:00:10.762
Controllers,

00:00:11.032 --> 00:00:14.962
badge readers and cameras were plugged
into whatever cable was nearest.

00:00:15.427 --> 00:00:20.057
Today that cable usually leads to the
same network as the office laptops.

00:00:20.547 --> 00:00:25.017
Infection that starts in finance can
reach the machine that fills the bottles.

00:00:26.183 --> 00:00:28.733
Second, these devices cannot be patched.

00:00:29.108 --> 00:00:31.058
A controller gets an update never.

00:00:31.393 --> 00:00:34.663
The vendor is gone,
or an update means stopping production.

00:00:35.183 --> 00:00:39.693
Cameras and door controllers ship with
a factory password nobody changes.

00:00:40.123 --> 00:00:44.473
The choice is to isolate, replace early,
or accept that it is open.

00:00:44.758 --> 00:00:47.288
That is a business
decision with a price tag.

00:00:48.317 --> 00:00:50.187
Third, the vendor's back door.

00:00:50.492 --> 00:00:53.822
The vendor set up remote access years ago,
and it stayed.

00:00:54.182 --> 00:00:58.332
One password, shared by the whole
service team, valid around the clock,

00:00:58.642 --> 00:00:59.812
straight to the controllers.

00:01:00.297 --> 00:01:03.667
The maintenance contract
usually says nothing about it.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.480
Fourth, the damage is physical.

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:09.020
A changed setpoint overheats a vessel.

00:01:09.375 --> 00:01:14.145
The operator screen can show normal
values while the process is anything but.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.200
That is not restored from a backup.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:19.050
From 20 January 2027,

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:24.220
the Machinery Regulation requires
that connected devices cannot make new

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:25.470
machinery unsafe.

00:01:26.683 --> 00:01:27.893
What good looks like.

00:01:28.178 --> 00:01:30.768
A device inventory,
with an owner for each.

00:01:31.083 --> 00:01:34.733
A wall between office and plant,
with one monitored crossing.

00:01:35.113 --> 00:01:40.083
Vendor access only on request, through
a jump host that records the session.

00:01:40.368 --> 00:01:44.298
Plant monitoring, and offline
backups of every configuration.

00:01:44.608 --> 00:01:46.198
IEC 62443

00:01:46.848 --> 00:01:49.898
zones and conduits are
the common yardstick.

00:01:51.697 --> 00:01:52.907
A suggested first step.

00:01:53.192 --> 00:01:54.462
Ask two questions.

00:01:54.767 --> 00:01:58.157
Can a laptop in the office
reach the production line today?

00:01:58.522 --> 00:02:00.172
And who would notice if it did?

00:02:00.482 --> 00:02:02.952
If the answers are probably and nobody,

00:02:03.202 --> 00:02:06.232
the first control is a
line on a network diagram.

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:09.560
Four ways the plant stops.

00:02:09.805 --> 00:02:11.895
Draw the line between office and plant.

